Seong-Kook Shin wrote:

> When my pet accidentally cross a polymorph trap,
> it polymorphed to another monster such as orc,
> dragon, or lich (yeah!)

A lich isn't really that good of a pet: it's only moderate.

A lich *is* a good adversary, since against *you* it will use all kinds
of magic. Unfortunately, it won't use magic against other monster.

> If I understand correctly, when pets (dogs and cats) are starving,
> they are attacking me or becoming hostile.

And so will all starving pets.

> But when my pet is a lich, it would be fine, undead don't eat
> at all.

Indeed. Liches won't starve, and won't attack you as a consequence of
that. Liches may still become untame, though, for example when you
displace them into a trap too often, or when you leave them alone for
too long.

> But when my pet is a dragon, they have no interest no eating.
> Is it safe not to feed dragon? Or, is there any special way
> to feed them? I do not want to lose such a powerful ally ;-)

You don't have too feed eating pets: they will feed themselves, and
that's enough to keep them tame, usually.

Because of this, liches are even less preferrable: gradually, things may
happen that make them less tame, and there's no way to feed them, so
this process in irreversably. A dragon, on the opposite, is easily
brought back to a higher level of tameness, so the risk of it becoming
untame isn't that big.

What you're confused with is probably a pet's apport rating. If you
hand-feed your pet, instead of letting it get its own kills and eat
those, its apport rating will increase.

